The qutrit action preserves `omega_t`. It permutes the three factor Chern
classes, while the determinant-one rank-nine sum has degree zero. Therefore
all three factors have equal `omega_t` degree and hence each has degree zero.

For a proper saturated `G` in one rank-three factor, `rank(G)<=2`. Equations
(1)-(3) give

\[
 \deg_t(G)
 \le 2M-\frac23(t-1).                                  \tag{4}
\]

Thus every factor is `omega_t`-stable whenever

\[
 t>1+3M.                                                \tag{5}
\]

Because `M` is finite, the Archimedean property supplies an integer, and
therefore a rational number,

\[
 t_W\in\mathbb Z_{>0},\qquad t_W>1+3M.                 \tag{6}
\]

The same `t_W` works for all three factors. Their direct sum is
`omega_tW`-polystable. It is not asserted to be stable, because its three
degree-zero factor summands are genuine subobjects.

Equation (6) is a proof witness, not a selected numerical output or a free
physical parameter. A numerical `mu1` certificate is still required to print
